# Final feasibility portfolio

## 1. Fixed requirements

The system must:

- consume 10 kW continuously;
- remain at ambient temperature indefinitely;
- be sealed and silent;
- use no electricity for cooling;
- use no fans, pumps, moving parts, water, refrigerants, or phase-change materials;
- use no conductive metal fins, external heat sink, ventilation, or colder contacting material;
- operate inside a sealed room whose walls cannot conduct heat outward;
- contain no hidden reservoir, heat sink, exothermic reaction, or reinterpretation of the power requirement.

## 2. Explicit contradiction

The electronics convert their 10 kW input into heat:

\[
P_{\text{heat}}=10{,}000\ \mathrm{J/s}
\]

For steady ambient operation, the same 10 kW must leave the rack continuously:

\[
P_{\text{out}}=P_{\text{heat}}=10\ \mathrm{kW}
\]

But the stated boundaries eliminate every allowed output path:

- conduction is blocked by the room boundary;
- convection and ventilation are prohibited;
- fluid transport is prohibited;
- phase-change storage is prohibited;
- refrigeration is prohibited;
- colder sinks are prohibited;
- radiation cannot provide net transfer to surroundings at the same ambient temperature;
- finite thermal storage cannot support indefinite operation.

Thus the requirements simultaneously demand:

\[
P_{\text{out}}=10\ \mathrm{kW}
\quad\text{and}\quad
P_{\text{out}}=0
\]

That is the contradiction.

## 3. Thermodynamic consequence

The sealed room can absorb heat only temporarily. Its internal energy increases as:

\[
E(t)=10{,}000t
\]

Equivalent accumulation:

- 36 MJ per hour;
- 864 MJ per day;
- 240 kWh per day.

Without an external heat-rejection path, room and rack temperature must rise. Remaining at ambient indefinitely is impossible under conservation of energy and the second law.

## 4. Boundary re-anchor

No proposal may introduce or imply:

- a hidden heat reservoir;
- an unlisted material property that violates thermodynamics;
- negative-temperature or zero-entropy behavior;
- an exothermic or endothermic reaction as a loophole;
- radiative escape through an unspecified boundary;
- an unmentioned external sink;
- a redefinition of “continuous 10 kW”;
- a new physical law.

## 5. Final disposition

No physically valid cooling device exists within the stated constraints. Any purported design would necessarily relax a requirement, accumulate heat for only a finite time, or violate established thermodynamics.
